# .gitignore protects the repository. It does nothing for `docker build`,
# which sends the whole directory as build context regardless -- so a file
# git has never seen can still be baked into an image and shipped to a
# container an attacker is standing in.
#
# It was. The decoy image contained config/intel_keys.json with live
# VirusTotal, Google Safe Browsing and abuse.ch keys, and canary_tokens.json
# with the bait list. An attacker who read the second one knew every token to
# avoid; one who read the first got working API credentials.

# Secrets. Never in any image.
config/intel_keys.json
config/canary_tokens.json
config/profiles/
config/persona_snapshots/

# The generated decoy service config: it lists the credentials the decoy will
# accept. It is written fresh into the container by scripts/deploy_decoy.py at
# deploy time, so the copy on disk is vestigial -- but it was still being baked
# into the hunter image, which renders attacker-controlled content. A hunter
# that got compromised could read which logins the decoy honours, and that a
# decoy exists at all.
config/honeypots/

# Collected intelligence and prior sessions.
telemetry/
reports/
screenshots/
logs/
*.db
*.jsonl

# History and local state.
.git/
.gitignore
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.venv/
venv/

# Operator-side material. A hunted session has no use for the tests that
# describe every detection rule, or for the console that reads the verdicts.
tests/
docs/
*.md
needs_human_review.txt
urls.txt
targets.txt

# Captured malware samples: never in any image. (telemetry/ already excluded,
# named here so the intent survives if that line ever changes.)
telemetry/samples/
*.quar
